## How It Works

1. The application loads the homepage displaying popular or featured books.
2. Users can search for books using the search bar.
3. Search queries are sent to the Google Books API.
4. Retrieved book data is dynamically rendered using reusable React components.
5. Users can add books to their favorites list.
6. Authentication (if enabled) allows users to save personal preferences.

## Installation and Setup

Follow these steps to run Bookora locally:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Shadya05/Bookora.git
cd Bookora
npm install
npm start

The application will run on:

http://localhost:3000

## Usage

- Browse books by category from the homepage
- Search for books using keywords, title, or author
- Click on a book to view details
- Add books to favorites for later reference
- Log in to manage personalized data (if applicable)

## Learning Outcomes

- Hands-on experience with React components and hooks
- Practical API integration using fetch or Axios
- Managing application state and user interactions
- Designing responsive and modern UI layouts

## Future Enhancements

- Advanced filtering and sorting options
- Book recommendations based on user interests
- Dark mode support
- Pagination or infinite scrolling
- Backend integration for persistent user data

## Conclusion

Bookora is a practical and engaging project that demonstrates how React can be used to build modern, API-driven web applications. It is suitable for learning, showcasing frontend skills, and extending into a full-scale book discovery platform.


